=== L1 Plot and Structure: Eight Actors, Eighty Days ===
id: 3508efb3-d85d-484c-a7e7-39a88a4f20a7
slug: plot-and-structure-eight-actors-eighty-days
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:14.055183+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 20

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Eason's episodic chase across continents: how eight actors tell a global story.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">A Novel Becomes a Race Against the Clock</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Jules Verne published his adventure novel in 1872, at a moment when two technologies had just made circumnavigation in eighty days theoretically possible: the Suez Canal opened in 1869 and the American transcontinental railroad was completed in the same year. Verne imagined a precise English gentleman betting £20,000 at his gentlemen's club that he could complete the journey &mdash; and the result is a breathless chase spanning India, Hong Kong, Japan, San Francisco and the Atlantic. Laura Eason's 2013 stage adaptation, which premiered at the New Vic Theatre in Newcastle-under-Lyme in a theatre-in-the-round configuration, distils this chase into something even tighter: eight actors playing every role in the story, from Phileas Fogg himself to train conductors, customs officials, ship&rsquo;s captains and a herd of elephants. Understanding the <dfn class="term" data-def="A play built from a series of short, self-contained scenes rather than a continuous unfolding action.">episodic structure</dfn> of this adaptation is the starting point for all the staging and performance work that follows.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: where and when did Eason's stage adaptation of Around the World in 80 Days premiere, and in what theatre configuration?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Eason&rsquo;s adaptation premiered at the New Vic Theatre, Newcastle-under-Lyme in 2013, directed by Theresa Heskins, in a theatre-in-the-round configuration with a cast of eight actors multi-roling throughout.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Episodic Structure and the Chase Narrative</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Where a linear play builds one continuous action through rising and falling tension, episodic structure links a series of self-contained scenes whose energy comes from momentum &mdash; the audience is always aware of time ticking down and distance to be crossed. Eason borrows this structure directly from Verne, who organised his novel as a series of geographical episodes: London, Paris, Suez, Bombay, Calcutta, Hong Kong, Yokohama, San Francisco, New York, Liverpool, London again. Each episode introduces new obstacles, new minor characters, and new opportunities for the eight-actor ensemble to transform themselves and the stage space. The play opens at the <dfn class="term" data-def="The framing scene of the play in which Fogg bets his Reform Club companions £20,000 that he can circumnavigate the globe in 80 days.">Reform Club opening</dfn>, establishing Fogg&rsquo;s precision and the terms of the wager, and closes in London with the apparent failure and then the extraordinary reveal of the international date line.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6">From a staging perspective, episodic structure creates specific demands. Each scene transition must be rapid &mdash; a shift of a chair, a change of costume accessory, a change of accent &mdash; and the performers and director must find ways to signal clearly to the audience that we have moved from one continent to another without slowing the pace. A director working on this play in an <dfn class="term" data-def="A staging configuration in which the audience surrounds the performance area on all sides.">in-the-round</dfn> space, as the original production did, must ensure that <dfn class="term" data-def="The lines of vision from the audience to the performers, which determine where and how performers must be placed.">sightlines</dfn> allow every audience member to see the key physical images from every angle: a tableau of the Reform Club, the arc of an elephant crossing the stage, the moment a train accelerates through the ensemble&rsquo;s bodies.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>What makes episodic structure different from a linear three-act play?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n7">A linear play (like The Crucible) builds one sustained dramatic question &mdash; will Proctor confess? &mdash; through escalating pressure in a single community. In contrast, <em>Around the World in 80 Days</em> has a fresh dramatic question in every new location: will Fogg catch the next departure? Will Fix succeed in detaining him? Will Passepartout survive his mishap in the Hong Kong opium-house scene? This episodic rhythm suits physical-theatre companies because each episode is essentially a self-contained performance problem: how do eight actors turn themselves into an Indian marketplace, a Japanese acrobat troupe, or a Native American war party on the transcontinental railway? The episodic form invites creativity in every transition rather than demanding sustained psychological realism in one fixed setting.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n8">A common misconception students should avoid: episodic does not mean &ldquo;random&rdquo;. Every episode advances Fogg&rsquo;s race against the clock, reveals character under pressure, and raises the comic and dramatic stakes. The structure is tight &mdash; the countdown creates tension across every episode, even when individual scenes are played for comedy.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Multi-Roling and the Ensemble as Engine</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10"><dfn class="term" data-def="When one actor plays two or more different roles in the same production, typically with costume changes or physical transformation.">Multi-roling</dfn> is not simply an economic decision in this play &mdash; it is the central theatrical pleasure. Eight performers play dozens of parts. One actor might be a Reform Club gentleman in the opening scene, a Suez customs officer ten minutes later, a Bombay merchant shortly after, and then a ship&rsquo;s engineer in the Atlantic crossing. The audience&rsquo;s delight comes partly from recognising the transformation and partly from the skill of the performer who makes each figure feel utterly distinct. For a student writing as a performer, this means that every minor character must be performed with precision: a distinct posture, a specific walk, a clear change of vocal pitch, pace or accent to mark the transition.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n11">The eight-actor <dfn class="term" data-def="A group of performers of equal status who work together as a unified company, often sharing roles, narrating, and creating visual images collectively.">ensemble</dfn> also creates the physical world of the play. Bodies become a steam engine (performers in a line, arms pumping in unison, voices creating a mechanical rhythm), an elephant (one actor as the body, others as legs, the whole image emerging from choreographed physical trust), or the heaving Atlantic (performers shifting weight in synchrony, creating a wave motion across the stage). This use of bodies-as-objects and <dfn class="term" data-def="When a performer uses their body and imagination to suggest that a prop or object has become something else entirely.">object transformation</dfn> is characteristic of companies like Complicite and Frantic Assembly, whose physical-theatre practice underpins Eason&rsquo;s script.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name three things the eight-actor ensemble transforms into using physical theatre in Around the World in 80 Days.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The ensemble uses physical theatre to transform performers&rsquo; bodies into the world of the play &mdash; steam engines, ships, elephants, storms and crowds &mdash; making multi-roling and object transformation central staging methods rather than incidental features.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: is the play a costume drama requiring realistic sets?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">A significant student error is to treat this play as a costume drama requiring a literal, naturalistic set &mdash; a train carriage for the India scenes, a ship&rsquo;s deck for the Atlantic, an Indian temple for the rescue of Aouda. In fact the play&rsquo;s entire aesthetic is built on the idea that the imagination of performers and audience, not scenery, creates the world. A director who specifies a realistic Victorian set is misreading the form. <dfn class="term" data-def="A production style in which the stage presents a selective, symbolic or non-realistic representation of the world.">Stylised staging</dfn> is not a compromise here &mdash; it is the artistic intention. Chairs become railway carriages; rope becomes rigging; the ensemble&rsquo;s coordinated movement becomes the ocean. Examiners reward students who understand this and who can describe how specific design choices support the transformation rather than substitute for it.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Narrated Transitions and Direct Address</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Eason also employs a narration device: performers step out of the dramatic fiction to address the audience directly, setting the location, updating the countdown, and commenting on Fogg&rsquo;s progress. This <dfn class="term" data-def="When a performer speaks directly to the audience, stepping out of the dramatic fiction to address them as themselves.">direct address</dfn> functions similarly to Brecht&rsquo;s use of a narrator in epic theatre &mdash; it frames the action, prevents the audience from losing themselves in pure empathy, and invites them to watch the storytelling as storytelling. From a design perspective, lighting transitions between dramatic scenes and narrator moments can be achieved with a sharp change of <dfn class="term" data-def="The overall lighting condition at a given moment in a production, defined by colour, intensity, direction and angle.">lighting state</dfn>: for example, a warm, busy wash during action scenes and a tight top-light or single <dfn class="term" data-def="An ellipsoidal spotlight which projects a sharp-edged beam of light, allowing a precise pool of illumination to be placed on one performer.">profile lantern</dfn> on the narrator to isolate them from the rest of the stage, signalling to the audience that we have stepped outside the story.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n17">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">Eason&rsquo;s 2013 adaptation uses episodic structure to match Verne&rsquo;s globe-trotting novel, creating a continuous ticking clock that connects every scene &mdash; eight actors, multi-roling throughout, are the engine of this structure.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Multi-roling and object transformation are central theatrical pleasures, not just economic choices: the ensemble creates trains, ships, elephants and storms through stylised physical theatre rather than realistic scenery.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Direct address narration frames the action, and a director&rsquo;s choices about lighting state and staging configuration (in-the-round, ensuring sightlines for every audience member) shape how the audience experiences each episode.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n16">For questions asking you to analyse the playwright&rsquo;s intentions, the most common mistake is describing what happens in the play rather than explaining what the playwright wants the audience to think or feel at that moment. For a question about the episodic structure, always connect the structural choice to the audience experience: the episodic form keeps the clock ticking in the audience&rsquo;s mind, creating a cumulative momentum that a linear form could not sustain. For extended staging questions, you must go beyond structure and identify concrete staging choices &mdash; how would you physically mark each scene transition? Which lighting state signals a new location? How does the ensemble move to signal the steam engine? Name specific moments and specific technical choices. &ldquo;The lighting would change&rdquo; scores in the Developing band; &ldquo;a snap blackout followed by a cool blue wash from upstage cross-lights to suggest the open Atlantic&rdquo; is the Mastering band.</p>

