=== L4 Context: Verne in 1872, Eason in 2013 ===
id: 5220cc73-0693-4720-b658-9dcea53f42b2
slug: context-verne-in-1872-eason-in-2013
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:00.267429+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 19

--- DESCRIPTION ---
The Victorian novel, its 19th-century travel craze, and how a modern adaptation reframes it.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Two Worlds, One Journey</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Around the World in 80 Days exists in two historical moments simultaneously. Jules Verne published the novel in 1872, feeding a Victorian appetite for global adventure at the precise moment when industrial technology had made such adventure feasible. Laura Eason&rsquo;s stage adaptation premiered in 2013 at a theatre in the north of England, for a 21st-century audience well aware of what empire had meant and where the &ldquo;adventure&rdquo; of Victorian global reach had led. Understanding both contexts is essential not because examiners will ask you to reproduce biographical facts, but because context shapes every staging choice a modern production might make &mdash; from the style of the physical theatre to the way the ensemble performs the non-European characters. Both Verne&rsquo;s world and Eason&rsquo;s inform what happens on stage.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Jules Verne and the Victorian Moment</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Jules Verne (1828&ndash;1905) was a French novelist and one of the founders of adventure fiction as a genre. He was fascinated by science and technology, and his novels are populated by engineers, explorers, submarines and balloons &mdash; the machines of a world in which human ingenuity was transforming the possible. <em>Around the World in 80 Days</em> was published in 1872, serialised in a French newspaper before appearing as a novel. It arrived at a specific historical junction: the Suez Canal had opened in 1869, dramatically shortening the sea journey between Europe and India; the American transcontinental railroad, also completed in 1869, had linked the Atlantic and Pacific coasts. Together these two engineering achievements made Verne&rsquo;s premise credible &mdash; a journey that would have taken six months was now theoretically achievable in eighty days.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">The novel&rsquo;s popularity was also driven by a specifically Victorian cultural obsession with time. The Great Exhibition of 1851 had celebrated industrial progress and the mastery of nature through technology; by the 1870s, standardised railway time was replacing local time across Britain, the telegraph was sending messages faster than any human traveller, and the world was being divided into predictable time zones. Fogg&rsquo;s devotion to his schedule is not a character quirk &mdash; it is a satirical portrait of Victorian industrial modernity, where the precision of the timetable had become a cultural value.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: what two technologies opened in 1869 made Fogg's journey possible, and why was Victorian Britain so preoccupied with time?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Verne wrote his novel in 1872, shortly after the Suez Canal (1869) and the transcontinental railroad (1869) made circumnavigation in 80 days theoretically possible. The Victorian obsession with time, schedules and industrial progress gave Fogg&rsquo;s character its cultural meaning.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">The Novel's Colonial Assumptions</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Verne&rsquo;s novel is a product of French and European imperial culture. The world outside Europe functions as spectacular terrain for Fogg&rsquo;s determination. India, China, Japan and America appear as a series of exotic episodes, each offering a different flavour of drama or comedy. Verne writes with genuine curiosity about these places, but his gaze is consistently that of the European traveller: the cultures he describes are observed from the outside. This is not a unique failing of Verne &mdash; it was the dominant mode of 19th-century travel writing, in which the world was divided into the civilised (European) traveller and the colourful (non-European) spectacle. A modern student reading the novel will notice this immediately; a modern director staging the play must decide how to handle it.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>The genre of Victorian adventure fiction</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n9">Victorian adventure fiction as a genre typically features: a Western (usually British or French) protagonist whose ingenuity and stoicism are tested by the world; a loyal, comic companion who provides the audience&rsquo;s emotional reaction; a series of exotic locations each with its own distinctive &ldquo;character&rdquo;; and a resolution that confirms the protagonist&rsquo;s superiority through success. Verne&rsquo;s novel fits this template closely, which is why Eason&rsquo;s adaptation can engage with both the pleasure of the genre and the questions a modern audience brings to it. The physical-theatre form Eason writes for &mdash; eight actors explicitly constructing the world &mdash; is itself a kind of comment on the genre: by making the construction visible, the production invites the audience to watch the storytelling as storytelling, rather than being absorbed unquestioningly into the colonial adventure. This is the same principle as <dfn class="term" data-def="Brecht's term for a theatrical effect that prevents the audience losing themselves in empathy and instead encourages them to think critically about what they are seeing.">alienation effect</dfn> in epic theatre: the ensemble&rsquo;s visible transformations remind the audience that they are watching a story, not experiencing reality.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Laura Eason and the 2013 Stage Adaptation</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Laura Eason is an American playwright, known in Britain for her work with the New Vic Theatre in Newcastle-under-Lyme. Her adaptation was commissioned specifically for the New Vic&rsquo;s in-the-round space, directed by Theresa Heskins. This matters for staging: an <dfn class="term" data-def="A staging configuration in which the audience surrounds the performance area on all sides, requiring all performer choices to be visible from every direction.">in-the-round</dfn> space requires a very different approach from a <dfn class="term" data-def="A staging configuration in which the audience faces a stage framed by a large rectangular arch, with the performers and set visible as if through a picture frame.">proscenium arch</dfn>. There is no &ldquo;upstage&rdquo; as a place of exit or hiding; there is no wing space for surprise entrances; every costume change, every physical transformation, every <dfn class="term" data-def="The choreographed arrangement of performers on stage at any given moment.">blocking</dfn> choice must work from every angle. The play Eason wrote is inseparable from this production context: the episodic multi-roling structure, the physical-theatre vocabulary, the use of minimal set and maximum performer ingenuity, all emerge from the demands of that space.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Eason&rsquo;s adaptation is firmly in the tradition of <dfn class="term" data-def="A theatre-making approach that emphasises storytelling through physical movement, ensemble creation and imagination rather than text-heavy realistic staging.">physical theatre</dfn>. The influence of companies like Complicite (founded 1983) and Frantic Assembly (founded 1994) is visible in the script&rsquo;s demands: performers are asked to become not just multiple characters but multiple objects and environments. This ensemble physical-theatre tradition sits comfortably alongside the Brechtian concept of the visible storyteller &mdash; the performer who steps out to narrate, to comment, to address the audience directly, making the theatrical fiction visible rather than concealing it behind a naturalistic fourth wall.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n13" data-revision-tip="Cover this and name: which two physical-theatre companies are most relevant to Eason's staging approach, and what year did each form?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Eason&rsquo;s script draws on the physical-theatre traditions of Complicite (founded 1983) and Frantic Assembly (founded 1994), whose ensemble work &mdash; object transformation, bodies-as-environments, ensemble storytelling &mdash; underpins the play&rsquo;s staging demands.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How does context inform a modern director's choices?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n14">A student who understands both historical contexts can answer a context question with precision. For example: a 12-mark question asking how the historical context shapes a director&rsquo;s choices for the India scenes might draw on three facts &mdash; (1) Verne wrote these scenes from a colonial gaze; (2) Eason adapted the play for a post-colonial 21st-century audience; (3) the physical-theatre form (visible transformation, ensemble storytelling) allows a modern production to handle this material critically without pausing the adventure. The directorial choice that follows from this context might be: the ensemble perform Indian characters with specific, researched <dfn class="term" data-def="A physical attitude or stance that communicates a character's social status and role in a single, instantly readable physical image.">gestus</dfn> &mdash; a particular posture, a specific movement quality &mdash; that communicates genuine cultural specificity, not a tourist&rsquo;s impression. This is a contextually informed staging choice, which is exactly what examiners reward at the highest band.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n16">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">Verne wrote in 1872 when the Suez Canal and the transcontinental railroad had made circumnavigation in 80 days newly possible &mdash; Fogg&rsquo;s schedule-obsession reflects Victorian industrial culture, not personal eccentricity.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">Eason&rsquo;s 2013 adaptation for the New Vic&rsquo;s in-the-round space draws on the physical-theatre traditions of Complicite and Frantic Assembly &mdash; the in-the-round configuration directly shapes the play&rsquo;s staging demands.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Understanding both historical contexts allows a director to make informed choices about how to handle Verne&rsquo;s colonial gaze &mdash; using gestus, costume and soundscape to give non-European characters specificity and dignity.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n15">Context questions require you to connect historical or theatrical facts directly to staging choices &mdash; not simply to list them. A common mistake is writing two paragraphs about Victorian history and Verne&rsquo;s biography and then adding &ldquo;this would affect the director&rsquo;s choices&rdquo; without specifying how. The examiner needs to see the link made explicit: &ldquo;Because Verne wrote from within a colonial culture, a modern director staging the India scenes would need to make deliberate choices to give Indian characters their own specificity &mdash; for example, using a researched gestus for each character rather than generic physical caricature.&rdquo; That is a contextually grounded staging answer. For the 12-mark analyse-intentions question, always move from context to intention to specific staging choice to audience effect &mdash; four steps, every time.</p>

