=== L8 Practitioner Application: Physical Theatre and Ensemble Storytelling ===
id: 66afea39-0e09-4db0-9fe6-b4ca0b721578
slug: practitioner-application-physical-theatre-and-ensemble-storytelling
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:12:59.818269+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 22

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Frantic Assembly, Complicite and the storytelling traditions a director borrows to stage Verne.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Practitioners Matter for This Play</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">When examiners ask students to apply a practitioner&rsquo;s techniques to a set play, they are not asking for a biography. They want to see students understand how a specific working method &mdash; a way of creating scenes, rehearsing the ensemble, constructing images or relating to the audience &mdash; could shape the choices a modern company makes when staging this text. For <em>Around the World in 80 Days</em>, three practitioner traditions are directly relevant and practically applicable: <dfn class="term" data-def="A physical theatre company founded in 1994 by Scott Graham and Steven Hoggett, known for high-energy ensemble work, lifts, contact choreography and collaboratively devised movement.">Frantic Assembly</dfn>, <dfn class="term" data-def="A theatre company founded by Simon McBurney in 1983, known for object transformation, storytelling ensemble work, narrator-led staging and precise physical imagery.">Complicite</dfn>, and the <dfn class="term" data-def="A comic performance tradition, particularly popular in Victorian-era variety entertainment, that emphasises direct audience address, comic precision, timing, song and shared theatrical pleasure.">music-hall and circus tradition</dfn> that the play&rsquo;s comic form echoes.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name the three most relevant practitioner traditions for this play and identify one specific technique from each that could be applied to a named moment.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The three most relevant practitioner traditions are: Frantic Assembly (ensemble physical theatre, contact work, collaborative image-making), Complicite (object transformation, storytelling ensemble, narrator-led staging) and the music-hall/circus tradition (direct audience address, comic precision, shared theatrical pleasure).</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Frantic Assembly: Bodies as Storytelling Instruments</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Frantic Assembly, founded by Scott Graham and Steven Hoggett in 1994, built its reputation on a form of physical theatre in which the body is not decorative but dramaturgical &mdash; the body&rsquo;s movement tells the story and creates the emotional world of the play. Their signature vocabulary includes <dfn class="term" data-def="A choreographic method developed by Frantic Assembly in which pairs of performers create movement by taking turns to respond to each other's physical initiation, building shared physical language.">round-by-through</dfn> exercises, <dfn class="term" data-def="Contact work in which performers manipulate, support or move each other&rsquo;s bodies, creating images that could not be achieved alone and communicating vulnerability, trust or power.">chair duets and contact choreography</dfn>, and lifts that serve narrative rather than spectacle.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6">In <em>Around the World in 80 Days</em>, Frantic Assembly&rsquo;s influence is most visible in the sequences where the company must become a single creature or vehicle. The elephant sequence, the train sequence, the ship &mdash; all require the same principle Frantic Assembly built their method on: the whole is more than the sum of its parts, and the audience reads a collective physical decision as a single image. A director working from Frantic Assembly&rsquo;s approach would develop these sequences in rehearsal through physical improvisation, allowing the company to find the movement together rather than having it choreographed from outside.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Applying Frantic Assembly&rsquo;s Methods to the Elephant Sequence</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n7"><strong>As a performer</strong> working in a Frantic Assembly-influenced rehearsal for the elephant sequence, you might begin the devising process with a round-by-through exercise in which two performers in physical contact with each other explore the rhythm and weight of a large, slow-moving creature. The goal is not to mime an elephant &mdash; it is to find the <dfn class="term" data-def="The quality of movement, particularly its relationship to the ground, often described in terms of how light or heavy a performer&rsquo;s steps and gestures feel.">weight and rhythm</dfn> in your own body so the physical choice becomes instinctive. Your vocal contribution in this scene is silence: the physical precision carries the full dramatic load.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n8"><strong>As a lighting designer</strong> for a Frantic Assembly-influenced staging of this sequence, you would trust the physical storytelling and resist the temptation to overilluminate. A warm amber wash from a high angle (suggesting the Indian sun), perhaps a <dfn class="term" data-def="A lighting state in which the intensity is reduced to a very low level, creating shadow and mystery.">dim state</dfn> with a gobo of leaf-shadow patterns projected onto the floor &mdash; giving the ensemble&rsquo;s bodies maximum visual impact against a subtly textured background &mdash; would be the Frantic Assembly aesthetic: clean, powerful, serving the movement without competing with it.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Complicite: Object Transformation and the Narrator</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Complicite (founded 1983, still directed by Simon McBurney) built a different but equally relevant tradition. Where Frantic Assembly&rsquo;s strength is in the physical ensemble, Complicite&rsquo;s signature is in <dfn class="term" data-def="The theatrical technique of using a prop or simple object to represent multiple things during a performance, the &lsquo;transformation&rsquo; happening through the performer&rsquo;s physical use of the object rather than through its appearance.">object transformation</dfn> and in the figure of the storytelling narrator. In Complicite&rsquo;s productions, company members regularly step in and out of the fiction to address the audience directly, to handle and transform objects, and to remind the audience that they are watching a story being told, not a reality being reproduced.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n11">This is exactly the theatrical mode Eason&rsquo;s script requires. The Complicite-influenced director would pay close attention to the way objects change meaning during the play: a chair is never just a chair once the ensemble has trained itself to use it as a carriage; a rope is never just a rope once it has been ship rigging. The discipline is that the transformation must be total and committed &mdash; there is no half-way position where the audience is uncertain whether this is a chair or a carriage. Complicite&rsquo;s training method emphasises that the object&rsquo;s transformation is effected entirely by the performer&rsquo;s physical relationship to it.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Close this and explain in two sentences how Complicite's approach to object transformation applies to a specific moment in Around the World in 80 Days.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Complicite&rsquo;s core principle: the object&rsquo;s meaning is entirely created by the performer&rsquo;s physical use of it. A chair becomes a train carriage the moment the performer sits in it as if in a moving carriage &mdash; not because the chair looks like a carriage, but because the body&rsquo;s logic makes it one.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Brechtian Elements: The Narrator and the Episodic Frame</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Bertolt Brecht&rsquo;s theatrical principles also have limited but specific relevance here. Brecht&rsquo;s <dfn class="term" data-def="Brecht's term for a suite of theatrical techniques designed to prevent the audience from losing themselves in the drama, encouraging critical thinking instead of passive emotional identification.">Verfremdungseffekt</dfn> (alienation effect) &mdash; using narration, direct address and visible theatricality to remind the audience they are watching a construction &mdash; is built into Eason&rsquo;s structure. Every time a company member steps forward to narrate the passage of days or comment on Fogg&rsquo;s progress, they perform a Brechtian function: the story is held at arm&rsquo;s length so the audience can observe it.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">The episodic structure &mdash; short scenes in clearly labelled locations, no attempt at continuous realistic time &mdash; is also Brechtian in spirit. Brecht&rsquo;s epic theatre avoided the seamless illusion of naturalism in favour of a structure that acknowledged each scene as a discrete unit. However, important to note: <em>Around the World in 80 Days</em> is not a Brechtian play in the full sense &mdash; it does not make political-didactic demands on its audience in the way <em>The Caucasian Chalk Circle</em> does. The Brechtian elements are structural tools within a comedy-of-adventure, not an ideological programme.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Music Hall and Circus: The Comedy Tradition Behind This Play</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n16">Victorian music hall &mdash; the variety entertainment tradition that was the dominant popular theatre form when Verne&rsquo;s novel was published &mdash; operated on principles that resonate strongly with Eason&rsquo;s play. Direct audience address was the norm; performers acknowledged the presence of the audience and played to them explicitly. Comic precision &mdash; the exact timing of a pratfall, the perfectly judged pause before a punchline &mdash; was a highly valued craft skill. Songs, physical spectacle, and an awareness that the audience&rsquo;s pleasure was the goal rather than their passive observation were all central.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n17">Some productions of this play interpolate live music &mdash; a piano onstage, a percussionist tracking the journey &mdash; that explicitly invokes this tradition. A director drawing on music-hall principles would coach Passepartout in particular on the shared, audience-aware quality of his comedy: the moment when he turns to the audience with an expression of disbelief, or when he makes an aside directly to the front row, is the music-hall gesture surviving into 21st-century ensemble theatre. In terms of <dfn class="term" data-def="The relationship between performers and audience, including whether the audience is positioned as passive observers or active participants in the theatrical event.">performer-audience relationship</dfn>, this play operates much closer to the music-hall end of the spectrum than to the fourth-wall naturalism of Stanislavski.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n19">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Frantic Assembly&rsquo;s ensemble physical theatre tradition &mdash; collaborative devising, contact choreography, bodies-as-storytelling-instruments &mdash; is directly applicable to the play&rsquo;s most demanding ensemble sequences (the elephant, the train, the ship).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Complicite&rsquo;s object transformation principle (an object&rsquo;s meaning is created entirely by the performer&rsquo;s physical use of it) and its narrator-led storytelling mode are built into the structure Eason has written.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">The music-hall tradition, with its direct audience address, comic precision and shared theatrical pleasure, explains the quality of the performer-audience relationship the play requires &mdash; far from Stanislavskian fourth-wall naturalism.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">When applying a practitioner to a set play, the most common error is to describe the practitioner&rsquo;s biography or general philosophy without showing how a specific technique applies to a specific moment of the play. The examiner wants you to say: &ldquo;Using Frantic Assembly&rsquo;s contact choreography approach, the director would develop the elephant sequence through physical improvisation in rehearsal, allowing the ensemble to find a shared weight and rhythm that makes the creature credible &mdash; the physical logic of the movement, not a visual representation, persuades the audience.&rdquo; That is a practitioner applied to a moment. Compare: &ldquo;Frantic Assembly like physical theatre&rdquo; &mdash; that is biography, not application. For the 8-mark questions in this lesson, make sure your performer or designer response also demonstrates how the practitioner&rsquo;s principles shape the specific choices you make.</p>

