  "text": "As a director drawing on the traditions of Frantic Assembly, Complicite and the music-hall form, explain how you would stage the opening Reform Club scene and the moment the wager is made. Cover your approach to performer direction, staging configuration, lighting, sound and the performer-audience relationship.",
  "type": "20 marks — Extended Staging Response",
  "marks": "Mastering: Full directorial vision drawing coherently on the three practitioner traditions. Frantic Assembly: the physical precision of the Reform Club ensemble — how the company moves as a social unit (shared weight, status physicality, a Frantic Assembly-devised movement score for the club's rituals). Complicite: the narrator stepping forward to address the audience; objects already becoming symbols (the chairs as club furniture, the pocket watch as the play's central image). Music hall: the awareness of the audience's presence even in this early scene; perhaps a direct address to the front row at the moment the wager is proposed, recruiting the audience as judge. Staging configuration: the choice of formation for the Reform Club (upstage semicircle with Fogg downstage, OR a cluster with Fogg central); the lighting state (precise, formal, slightly cold for London precision); sound (a clock ticking, perhaps a distant locomotive whistle as the wager is made, foreshadowing). The performer-audience relationship is explicitly addressed. Every choice has a named effect. A directorial statement about the overall intended experience.\n\nSecure: Most disciplines covered, with at least two of the three practitioner traditions meaningfully applied. Concrete choices. Some audience effect.\n\nDeveloping: Some disciplines covered; practitioner references are general rather than specific to the moment. Effects implied.\n\nEmerging: Basic description of the scene with little practitioner application or design specifics."
 }
]

