C:\Users\tshau\Documents\Study Vault\scripts\_retrofc\units\drama-aqa__around-the-world-80-days\raw\L01.txt:172: "q": "In what year was Eason's stage adaptation of Around the World in 80 Days first performed?"
C:\Users\tshau\Documents\Study Vault\scripts\_retrofc\units\drama-aqa__around-the-world-80-days\raw\L01.txt:176: "q": "Who directed the world premiere of Around the World in 80 Days at the New Vic?"
C:\Users\tshau\Documents\Study Vault\scripts\_retrofc\units\drama-aqa__around-the-world-80-days\raw\L01.txt:180: "q": "What staging configuration was used in the New Vic premiere?"
C:\Users\tshau\Documents\Study Vault\scripts\_retrofc\units\drama-aqa__around-the-world-80-days\raw\L04.txt:12: <p data-narration-id="n2">Around the World in 80 Days exists in two historical moments simultaneously. Jules Verne published the novel in 1872, feeding a Victorian appetite for global adventure at the precise moment when industrial technology had made such adventure feasible. Laura Eason&rsquo;s stage adaptation premiered in 2013 at a theatre in the north of England, for a 21st-century audience well aware of what empire had meant and where the &ldquo;adventure&rdquo; of Victorian global reach had led. Understanding both contexts is essential not because examiners will ask you to reproduce biographical facts, but because context shapes every staging choice a modern production might make &mdash; from the style of the physical theatre to the way the ensemble performs the non-European characters. Both Verne&rsquo;s world and Eason&rsquo;s inform what happens on stage.</p>
C:\Users\tshau\Documents\Study Vault\scripts\_retrofc\units\drama-aqa__around-the-world-80-days\raw\L04.txt:36: <h2 data-narration-id="n10">Laura Eason and the 2013 Stage Adaptation</h2>
